    <!-- Title -->
    <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white mb-4">
      Why Metadata Removal Matters
    </h2>

    <!-- Story Description -->
    <div class="space-y-4 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-400 leading-[1.65]">
      <p class="text-slate-200">
        Imagine snapping a photo of your morning coffee on your apartment balcony and sharing it online. To your friends, it’s just a casual post. But embedded deep inside the file’s EXIF tags is a silent surveillance record: the exact latitude and longitude pointing directly to your building, an altitude value revealing which floor you live on, your smartphone’s unique factory serial number, and a timestamp recorded down to the millisecond.
      </p>

      <p>
        Without metadata removal, anyone downloading that image — from automated forum scrapers to malicious actors — can reverse-engineer those coordinates with street mapping software. Over time, recurring photos shared across public communities allow strangers to track your daily routine, home address, and device footprint.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong class="text-white font-medium">CleanPhoto</strong> solves this locally. Before your photo ever reaches an upload field, our client-side engine paints the visual pixels onto an offscreen canvas and re-encodes the image fresh in your browser memory. All GPS coordinates, device fingerprints, and serial numbers are permanently destroyed — ensuring you share only the picture, never your identity.
      </p>
    </div>

